"""Original institutional and inclusive-administration exercises."""


def build(c, m):
    return c(74, 'Decentralization and Inclusive Public Administration', 'Decentralization\nand Inclusive\nPublic Administration',
        'Analyse a fictional distribution of public-service authority, resources and accountability, and identify inclusive delivery improvements within verified mandates.', [
        m('Map actual authority and resources', 'Distinguish a decentralisation label from practical decision powers and delivery capacity.', [
            'Decentralisation describes changes in where public decisions and responsibilities sit, but a label does not establish what a particular office can do. Ask who sets policy, allocates money, employs staff, chooses delivery arrangements and answers for results. These functions may be distributed differently across institutions. A secondary review of African decentralisation highlights implementation questions about resources and accountability. Its accessible indexed sections support contextual questions, while incomplete verification of its selection methods limits stronger claims. Do not infer the constitutional position of any country from a broad regional description. [R63]',
            'In the fictional service, draw separate entries for a central unit, a district office and a community contact point. For each, distinguish a written responsibility from an actual decision, resource or response that the supplied records demonstrate. An office may receive enquiries but lack authority to change eligibility. Another may hold a budget while depending on a separate staffing decision. Describe that mismatch without concluding that every local arrangement is ineffective. The purpose is to find which dependency needs verification, rather than to endorse centralisation or decentralisation as a universal solution. [R63]',
            'Accountability requires knowing both who can act and who must explain. The UN institutional synthesis describes the importance of mandates and follow-up in public accountability. A learner should therefore identify the role that can clarify a disputed responsibility and the evidence needed to confirm it. A local contact point should not be blamed for a decision controlled elsewhere, but lack of control should not become an excuse for an unexplained referral. Distinguish acknowledging a question, transmitting it to a verified office and obtaining a response that addresses the issue. [S54]',
            'Write a bounded institutional map using the invented organisational note and budget extract. Mark any unclear power as unverified, specify the relevant record and propose a responsible clarification role. Do not insert assumed Nigerian legal provisions, claim an agency has delegated a power or use real personal complaints to test your map. A useful map can show a practical obstacle and a feasible next check even when the legal position remains outside the supplied evidence. Its value lies in transparent reasoning about authority, resources and responsibility. [S54]'],
            'A fictional district desk must resolve access problems but cannot change service hours or purchase materials. The central unit controls both decisions; neither office explains referrals.',
            'Map three functions, their decision owners and resource dependencies. Identify one responsibility mismatch and a verification question.',
            'The local responsibility exceeds the powers demonstrated in the case. Clarify decision ownership and referral follow-up without inventing delegated authority.', ['R63', 'S54'], [
                ('Institutional labels do not establish practical powers.', 'A desk is called decentralised. What must still be checked?', 'Its actual authority, resources and accountability.', ['That it controls every budget.', 'That all decisions are now local.', 'That no central responsibilities remain.'], 'The label alone does not specify a distribution of functions.'),
                ('Responsibility and authority can be misaligned.', 'A fictional desk must change opening hours but has no power to do so. What is the issue?', 'A mismatch between assigned responsibility and demonstrated authority.', ['Proof that every local office is corrupt.', 'A reason to invent legal powers.', 'Evidence that opening hours never matter.'], 'The task depends on a decision held elsewhere.'),
                ('Regional reviews require contextual limits.', 'How should the indexed African decentralisation review be used here?', 'To frame contextual implementation questions with stated access limits.', ['As the constitution of every African country.', 'As a verified pooled causal estimate.', 'As proof that one model always works.'], 'The inspected synthesis does not support universal legal or causal claims.'),
                ('Referral and resolution are separate.', 'A fictional desk forwards a question. What remains to be demonstrated?', 'An appropriate receiving role and a response addressing the issue.', ['Automatic resolution at the moment of sending.', 'A right for the learner to decide the claim.', 'That the central unit necessarily rejected it.'], 'Transmission alone does not establish receipt, action or resolution.'),
                ('Unverified powers should remain visible.', 'An invented organisation note omits procurement authority. What should the map show?', 'Authority unverified, with the record and role needed for clarification.', ['An assumed power based on the office name.', 'A fabricated statutory section.', 'A personal accusation without records.'], 'Explicit uncertainty is more accurate than a guessed mandate.')]),
        m('Design inclusive administrative access', 'Identify barriers in a fictional service journey and propose proportionate alternatives.', [
            'An administrative service can appear open while its practical requirements prevent some intended users from reaching a response. Review the journey from finding information to understanding a decision: language, format, timing, transport, digital access and the ability to obtain an explanation can each matter. Participation guidance asks institutions to consider who is missing and whether chosen methods fit the purpose and available resources. Apply that concern to the invented service journey, without assuming that attendance at one meeting or use of one application represents all intended users. [S53]',
            'Describe a barrier in relation to a specific task. A form that can only be completed during working hours may obstruct a person with inflexible work; an image without readable text can obstruct access through assistive tools. A proposed alternative must preserve a functioning response, not just provide another front door. Identify staffing, a responsible role, accessible information and a way to explain the next step. Avoid assigning a fixed preference or ability to a demographic group. The case supplies fictional access circumstances, not a licence to stereotype real residents. [S53]',
            'Administrative fairness also concerns understandable reasons and a verified route for further consideration. Distinguish an enquiry about a record from a complaint about conduct and from a formal review of a decision. Their authority, evidence and procedures may differ, and local operating rules require confirmation. Public accountability depends on institutional roles and follow-up. The learner can propose what needs checking and who should clarify it, but cannot promise a legal remedy, determine a real application or solicit sensitive case histories to make the exercise more realistic. [S54]',
            'Compare alternatives using the stated purpose and constraints. An assisted channel may address a digital barrier but fail if nobody processes its submissions; longer hours may help one route while requiring staffing that the plan lacks. Record the expected benefit, resource dependency and observation needed to see whether the alternative works. Include a feedback step that explains the decision and any unresolved limit. Inclusive administration is an ongoing delivery question supported by evidence, rather than a claim that one format or one consultation has permanently removed every barrier. [S53]'],
            'A fictional service accepts only an image-based online form between nine and four. A proposed telephone alternative has no processing role and cannot explain decisions.',
            'Identify two task-specific barriers. Compare two alternatives, including the resources and feedback needed for each.',
            'Alternatives must connect usable access to an actual response. Avoid treating a new contact channel as a completed service or a universal solution.', ['S53', 'S54'], [
                ('Access barriers concern tasks and circumstances.', 'Which description is most precise for the fictional image-only form?', 'It may prevent a user of text-reading tools from completing the task.', ['Every person in a demographic category lacks digital skills.', 'Online publication proves universal access.', 'The form is unlawful in every jurisdiction.'], 'The statement identifies a task-specific barrier without stereotyping or inventing law.'),
                ('Alternatives require an operating response.', 'What must accompany the invented telephone channel?', 'A responsible processing role and an understandable next step.', ['Only a publicity announcement.', 'A guarantee that every claim will succeed.', 'Removal of all records of decisions.'], 'A usable contact route must connect to accountable processing.'),
                ('Review routes have different purposes and authority.', 'Why distinguish an enquiry from a formal decision review?', 'Their remit, evidence and authorised procedures may differ.', ['They always produce identical remedies.', 'A learner may determine either without authority.', 'No operating rule needs checking.'], 'Different routes should not be merged into an unsupported promise.'),
                ('Alternative designs have resource dependencies.', 'A proposed extended-hours desk lacks staff. What should the comparison record?', 'The staffing dependency and how it would be verified.', ['That longer hours are already fully implemented.', 'That an announcement creates capacity.', 'That no alternative can ever help.'], 'A proposed access benefit depends on an operating arrangement.'),
                ('Inclusion should be evaluated rather than assumed.', 'One fictional meeting is well attended. What remains unknown?', 'Which perspectives are missing and whether the service journey is usable.', ['That every intended user participated.', 'That all barriers have disappeared.', 'That no further feedback is necessary.'], 'Attendance at one event does not establish universal inclusion.')]),
        m('Coordinate responsibilities and review bottlenecks', 'Propose a traceable coordination correction without confusing activities with outcomes.', [
            'Services often depend on several institutions even when the public sees a single programme name. A coordination problem arises when a necessary handover lacks a receiving role, a clear task or a way to confirm progress. Map the fictional sequence and identify what each institution controls. Regional decentralisation questions can help reveal a mismatch between stated responsibility and practical resources, but they cannot establish that every intergovernmental arrangement has the same defect. Use the case records to locate a particular bottleneck rather than attach a broad diagnosis to an entire system. [R63]',
            'A handover should specify the non-personal information needed, the receiving role, the action requested and a check that the request reached the appropriate place. This exercise uses invented records only. Do not add personal complaints, circulate identities or create an unofficial parallel register. Institutional accountability includes a response to recommendations and clarification of mandates. Where responsibilities overlap, propose an authorised clarification instead of assigning new powers yourself. A coordination meeting can help agree a process, but the meeting itself is not evidence that an outstanding service problem has been resolved. [S54]',
            'Choose a review measure that relates to the identified bottleneck. If the issue is unacknowledged transfers, count comparable fictional transfers with confirmed receipt and an owner, alongside the time period and denominator. That is a process measure, not a final assessment of public wellbeing. The OECD distinguishes institutional outputs from subsequent outcomes and influence. A new coordination note may be a completed action while effective service access remains uncertain. Report both states rather than treating a signed memorandum or a reduced inbox count as proof of universal service improvement. [S55]',
            'Recommend a small correction with a responsible decision role, a resource requirement and a review point. Explain which observation would support continuation, modification or reconsideration of the arrangement. A different office may need to authorise resources, and a changed demand pattern may affect the apparent result. State those dependencies and alternatives so the recommendation can be examined honestly. The learner output is an analytical proposal grounded in the fictional sequence; it is neither an intergovernmental agreement nor permission to operate outside a verified institutional mandate. [S55]'],
            'Three fictional offices each record a referral as complete when sent. None checks receipt. A new coordination meeting is reported as proof that all outstanding enquiries are resolved.',
            'Design a four-field handover note and a process measure with denominator. Rewrite the success statement and identify a decision role for a limited correction.',
            'Specify receiving role, task, acknowledgement and follow-up status. A meeting or sent message cannot substitute for evidence of receipt, processing and an appropriate response.', ['R63', 'S54', 'S55'], [
                ('A bottleneck should be located in an actual sequence.', 'The fictional referral process ends at sending. What bottleneck is demonstrated?', 'No confirmation of an appropriate receiving role or receipt.', ['Proof that every institution is unnecessary.', 'A guarantee that recipients are ignoring requests deliberately.', 'A reason to publish personal complaints.'], 'The supplied process lacks a verified handover step.'),
                ('Coordination activities differ from resolved services.', 'What does a fictional coordination meeting establish by itself?', 'That a meeting occurred, without proving all enquiries were resolved.', ['Universal resolution.', 'A new statutory mandate.', 'Measured long-term wellbeing gains.'], 'An activity record does not demonstrate a final service result.'),
                ('Process measures need a denominator and period.', 'Which measure fits the fictional handover bottleneck?', 'Confirmed appropriate receipts out of all comparable transfers in the period.', ['The most flattering count without scope.', 'Total meeting participants as universal access.', 'Names of real complainants.'], 'A defined denominator and period make the process observation interpretable.'),
                ('Mandate conflicts require authorised clarification.', 'Two invented offices each deny responsibility for the same step. What is proportionate?', 'Request clarification from a role authorised to settle that responsibility.', ['Create a new power through a learner worksheet.', 'Assume the nearest desk controls everything.', 'Hide the unresolved dependency.'], 'A classroom proposal cannot allocate legal powers.'),
                ('Review should distinguish correction and outcome.', 'A handover note is adopted but service access has not been checked. What should the report say?', 'The process change is documented; access outcomes remain unverified.', ['Every intended user now receives service.', 'The note caused every later improvement.', 'No further observation can add value.'], 'Implementation evidence and outcome evidence answer different questions.')])])
